---
/** Distinguish actor-local allocation from independently collected VM heaps. */
---

<div class="memory-profile-grid" aria-label="Oreslang backend memory-management goals">
  <article class="memory-profile memory-profile-native">
    <div class="memory-profile-header">
      <span class="memory-profile-kicker">LLVM · Native</span>
      <strong class="memory-profile-goal">Zero GC</strong>
    </div>
    <h4>Actor-owned native arenas with no tracing collector.</h4>
    <p>
      Native actor-local slabs, bounded arenas and generational handles are
      designed for deterministic release at validated lifecycle boundaries.
      Ownership and borrowing, quiescence and FFI checks must make every lifetime
      safe without introducing tracing GC.
    </p>
    <p class="memory-profile-foot">Design target: LLVM does not ship an allocator or collector for Oreslang; these native arenas are runtime work, and full guest lowering remains unfinished. Zero GC does not mean zero allocation.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="memory-profile memory-profile-managed">
    <div class="memory-profile-header">
      <span class="memory-profile-kicker">JVM / GraalVM · Managed + native</span>
      <strong class="memory-profile-goal">Minimal GC</strong>
    </div>
    <h4>Actor-owned FFM arenas, plus managed GC where needed.</h4>
    <p>
      Java's Foreign Function &amp; Memory API supports native arenas with
      deterministic close. Oreslang's actor-local memory slices and FFM
      prototypes aim to reduce heap churn, alongside escape analysis,
      allocation elimination and actor-local reuse.
    </p>
    <p class="memory-profile-foot">Per-actor arenas are not separate JVM GC heaps: Java wrappers, actor mailboxes and Truffle objects still participate in the enclosing VM/isolate collector. Fewer allocations may reduce pressure, not guarantee zero pauses.</p>
  </article>
</div>
